

<% content_for :title, "Buddhism & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<style>
  .buddhism-bipolar-page .buddhism-copy {
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .buddhism-bipolar-page .buddhism-source {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.75em;
    font-style: normal;
  }

  .buddhism-bipolar-page .buddhism-transformation {
    margin-block: 2rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.8;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>

<main class="buddhism-bipolar-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="buddhism-bipolar-hero">

    <div class="buddhism-bipolar-hero-content">

      <p class="buddhism-bipolar-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / BUDDHISM
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>BUDDHIST LENS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="buddhism-bipolar-intro">
        YOU ARE EXPERIENCING THE STORM.<br>
        YOU ARE NOT THE FUCKING STORM.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>

  <!-- 01 / BUDDHIST PERSPECTIVE ON BIPOLAR DISORDER -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-pink" aria-labelledby="buddhism-introduction-title">
    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">01 / BUDDHIST PERSPECTIVE</p>
      <h2 id="buddhism-introduction-title">BUDDHIST PERSPECTIVE ON BIPOLAR DISORDER</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">
      <blockquote>
        <p>“Though one should conquer a thousand men in battle a thousand times, yet he indeed is the noblest victor who would conquer himself.”</p>
        <cite class="buddhism-source">—Dhammapada, v. 103</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Reading the Dhammapada on a hard jail bunk gave me a sense of peace despite
        where I was. I began practicing its teachings and discovering how Buddhism could
        help me cope with bipolar disorder through awareness, compassion, and a
        different relationship with suffering.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 / THE FOUR NOBLE TRUTHS -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-yellow" aria-labelledby="buddhism-four-noble-truths-title">
    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">02 / BUDDHIST PERSPECTIVE</p>
      <h2 id="buddhism-four-noble-truths-title">THE FOUR NOBLE TRUTHS</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">
      <p>
        The Four Noble Truths begin by acknowledging suffering. Bipolar disorder can
        bring painful episodes, consequences, and fear of what comes next. Recognizing
        that pain gives you a place to begin, without treating your illness as
        punishment or personal failure.
      </p>

      <p>
        Craving, resistance, and self-judgment can add suffering around the illness.
        They do not cause bipolar disorder. Letting go of some of that struggle makes
        room for peace even while the condition remains.
      </p>

      <p>
        The Eightfold Path puts this into practice: understand your condition, act with
        compassion, speak honestly, take responsibility, choose supportive work, build
        sustainable habits, notice your mental state, and cultivate gentle attention.
      </p>

      <p class="buddhism-transformation">
        Recognize → Embrace → Understand → Respond → Transform
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 / IMPERMANENCE -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-black" aria-labelledby="buddhism-impermanence-title">
    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">03 / BUDDHIST PERSPECTIVE</p>
      <h2 id="buddhism-impermanence-title">NOTHING IS PERMANENT</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">
      <p>
        Thoughts, feelings, energy, and circumstances change. Bipolar disorder may be
        lifelong, but your experience of it keeps changing, like water flowing through
        the same river. Impermanence invites you to appreciate periods of stability and
        remember during difficult periods that your current experience is not fixed
        forever.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 / NONSELF -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-purple" aria-labelledby="buddhism-nonself-title">
    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">04 / BUDDHIST PERSPECTIVE</p>
      <h2 id="buddhism-nonself-title">NONSELF</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">
      <p>
        Nonself invites you to loosen rigid labels about who you are. Your body,
        feelings, perceptions, mental habits, and awareness—the Five Aggregates—are
        changing processes. Mania, depression, and the things you regret do not describe
        your entire existence. You can take responsibility for your actions while
        remembering that you are more than an episode.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 / MINDFULNESS -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-pink" aria-labelledby="buddhism-mindfulness-title">
    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">05 / BUDDHIST PERSPECTIVE</p>
      <h2 id="buddhism-mindfulness-title">MINDFULNESS</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">
      <p>
        Mindfulness means noticing what is happening without immediately judging or
        reacting. Observe whether an experience feels pleasant, unpleasant, or neutral;
        notice the condition of your mind; and explore what feeds a pattern. “Anger is
        present right now” creates more space than “I am an angry person.” When
        possible, that space helps you choose your response.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 06 / ATTACHMENT, AVERSION, AND LETTING GO -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-yellow" aria-labelledby="buddhism-letting-go-title">
    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">06 / BUDDHIST PERSPECTIVE</p>
      <h2 id="buddhism-letting-go-title">ATTACHMENT, AVERSION, AND LETTING GO</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">
      <p>
        Resisting depression and demanding that you function like someone without
        bipolar disorder can add shame to an already painful experience. Letting go
        means loosening those expectations, accepting your present circumstances, and
        forgiving yourself. You can care about getting better while responding to
        yourself with kindness.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 07 / THE WARRIOR’S PATH -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-black" aria-labelledby="buddhism-warriors-path-title">
    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">07 / BUDDHIST PERSPECTIVE</p>
      <h2 id="buddhism-warriors-path-title">THE WARRIOR’S PATH</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">
      <p>
        The warrior’s path means meeting fear with courage, gentleness, and openness.
        You may fear another episode and still participate in life. Take reasonable
        precautions, accept support, and rebuild trust. Vulnerability can help you
        reconnect when fear makes you want to withdraw into your cocoon.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 08 / TRANSFORMING PROBLEMS INTO WISDOM -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-purple" aria-labelledby="buddhism-wisdom-title">
    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">08 / BUDDHIST PERSPECTIVE</p>
      <h2 id="buddhism-wisdom-title">TURNING PROBLEMS INTO WISDOM</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">
      <p>
        Painful experiences can deepen your understanding of yourself and others. You
        can learn from them without believing you deserved or needed them. Practice
        patience, repair relationships, challenge stigma, and use what you have learned
        to support others. Finding meaning does not require calling bipolar disorder a
        gift.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 09 / THE MIDDLE WAY: LIVING WITH BIPOLAR DISORDER -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-pink" aria-labelledby="buddhism-middle-way-title">
    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">
      <p class="buddhism-number">09 / BUDDHIST PERSPECTIVE</p>
      <h2 id="buddhism-middle-way-title">THE MIDDLE WAY: LIVING WITH BIPOLAR DISORDER</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">
      <p>
        The Middle Way offers a way to live with the condition through wisdom,
        compassion, and steady practice. Accept its realities while continuing to pursue
        stability, support, and a meaningful life. Acceptance leaves room for change.
        You can experience ordinary happiness and sadness without treating every feeling
        as evidence of an episode.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CLOSING -->
  <section class="buddhism-ending" aria-labelledby="buddhism-closing-title">
    <div>
      <p class="buddhism-ending-small">CLOSING</p>
      <h2 id="buddhism-closing-title">
        FIND SOME<br>
        <span>LIGHT.</span>
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-ending-copy">
      <p>
        Return to these teachings when you need some light: recognize what is happening,
        respond with compassion, and keep practicing.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "buddhism-back-button" %>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>


